1
00:00:12,912 --> 00:00:14,614
[engine starts, revving]

2
00:00:21,066 --> 00:00:23,366
[Jerry] Weird. Wild. Odd.

3
00:00:24,633 --> 00:00:27,466
This is a 1964 Studebaker Avanti...

4
00:00:27,666 --> 00:00:28,833
Avanti.

5
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:32,033
...in red. Interior: black vinyl.

6
00:00:33,866 --> 00:00:37,866
Studebaker was an American car company
that lasted until about 1967.

7
00:00:38,066 --> 00:00:39,966
The name tells you
everything you need to know.

8
00:00:40,233 --> 00:00:41,433
Studebaker.

9
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:45,866
They used to make
cars like the Lark, the Hawk.

10
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:47,566
They like birds, these people.

11
00:00:47,766 --> 00:00:50,000
And making sure their car
can go through a puddle.

12
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:53,199
And hit some straw.

13
00:00:53,699 --> 00:00:55,166
They didn't do well,

14
00:00:55,233 --> 00:00:58,166
so in a last ditch,
Hail Mary effort to save their business,

15
00:00:58,366 --> 00:01:01,933
they asked design genius Raymond Loewy
to make them a sports car,

16
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,866
which he did in a 40-day crash program
somewhere on the outskirts of Las Vegas.

17
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:12,500
He created the entire concept
in eight days on one condition.

18
00:01:12,899 --> 00:01:17,800
Studebaker was not allowed to change
one aspect of his original design.

19
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:20,133
Of course not. Look at this man.

20
00:01:20,333 --> 00:01:22,933
He's got a mustache,
two hankies, and a tie bar.

21
00:01:23,033 --> 00:01:25,266
You gonna argue with him
about how something should look?

22
00:01:25,333 --> 00:01:26,966
It is the Avanti.

23
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:30,566
People loved it. They wanted to buy it.

24
00:01:30,633 --> 00:01:34,333
But when they came in the showroom,
it wasn't there.

25
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:37,166
Studebaker couldn't figure out
how to build enough of them

26
00:01:37,266 --> 00:01:39,100
and they collapsed anyway.

27
00:01:39,332 --> 00:01:40,900
Nice try.

28
00:01:40,967 --> 00:01:41,901
[tires screech]

29
00:01:43,633 --> 00:01:46,433
It is also the favorite car of 
my wonderfully funny guest today, 

30
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:47,966
Mr. J.B. Smoove.

31
00:01:48,933 --> 00:01:53,566
But he has never been in one, never owned
one, never even had a ride in one.

32
00:01:53,966 --> 00:01:57,300
But "Avanti" means "Let's go!" So, we are.

33
00:01:58,500 --> 00:01:59,699
[phone ringing]

34
00:01:59,766 --> 00:02:01,300
-[J.B.] Hello?
-[Jerry] J.B.

35
00:02:01,366 --> 00:02:02,433
[Jerry] J.S.

36
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:04,699
You won't believe 
what I got for you today.

37
00:02:04,899 --> 00:02:08,000
[J.B.] I'm hoping you have 
what I think you have.

38
00:02:08,066 --> 00:02:10,366
[Jerry] All your dreams
come true today, J.B.

39
00:02:10,566 --> 00:02:12,866
[J.B.] I can't wait! 
I'm tingly! I'm tingly!

40
00:02:16,299 --> 00:02:21,566
I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is
Comedians In Cars Getting Coffee.

41
00:02:27,833 --> 00:02:29,799
Oh, this is beautiful.

42
00:02:30,566 --> 00:02:32,433
-Comedy brother.
-Comedy.

43
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:33,966
This comedy gold.

44
00:02:34,033 --> 00:02:37,166
They always say comedy gold.
Is there a silver and bronze also?

45
00:02:37,233 --> 00:02:38,433
That might as well be garbage.

46
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:39,500
Never even heard people

47
00:02:39,566 --> 00:02:40,633
-use the term...
-No.

48
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:42,733
...comedy bronze or comedy silver.

49
00:02:42,799 --> 00:02:44,366
-We don't trade in that.
-Always gold.

50
00:02:44,433 --> 00:02:45,466
Always gold.

51
00:02:47,766 --> 00:02:53,266
This is probably the nicest
Studebaker Avanti in the world.

52
00:02:53,366 --> 00:02:55,833
Are you kidding me?
You know what I love about this Avanti?

53
00:02:55,900 --> 00:03:00,466
It mixes European styling
with American ingenuity.

54
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:04,733
And this "S" also stands for "Smoove!"

55
00:03:05,366 --> 00:03:06,466
You can't beat this!

56
00:03:08,299 --> 00:03:10,566
Look at this steering wheel,
these little grips.

57
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:12,500
Now here's my favorite feature.

58
00:03:12,566 --> 00:03:13,600
Are you ready?

59
00:03:14,066 --> 00:03:15,700
This is for your lady.

60
00:03:16,533 --> 00:03:18,633
How much would your wife go nuts for that?

61
00:03:18,833 --> 00:03:22,299
Jerry, I'm telling you,
this is like Christmas.

62
00:03:22,335 --> 00:03:25,138
-Your birthday. Yeah.
-A car should feel like Christmas.

63
00:03:28,641 --> 00:03:31,678
[J.B.] Gonna tell you something.
A bloody nose can get you out of anything.

64
00:03:31,766 --> 00:03:34,833
If you carry a handkerchief
with red dye on it,

65
00:03:34,899 --> 00:03:36,866
you go, "Oh!"
You tap your nose a few times.

66
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:38,233
Like that.

67
00:03:38,284 --> 00:03:40,787
You pull that handkerchief out,
you don't let them see the red.

68
00:03:40,866 --> 00:03:43,700
If you go like this,
they will panic with you.

69
00:03:43,766 --> 00:03:44,766
And you go like this.

70
00:03:44,833 --> 00:03:46,966
-"Excuse me, I have a bloody nose."
-That's so funny.

71
00:03:47,033 --> 00:03:49,000
You are excused from anything.

72
00:03:49,133 --> 00:03:52,133
Court, you get pulled over,
by the police, pull you over.

73
00:03:52,333 --> 00:03:55,100
-Right.
-Everybody respects a bloody nose, Jerry.

74
00:03:59,299 --> 00:04:01,033
People who love J.B.,

75
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:02,966
-love J.B.
-Yeah.

76
00:04:03,009 --> 00:04:05,979
Do you feel comfortable
calling yourself by your name?

77
00:04:06,045 --> 00:04:07,814
I love putting myself in the third person.

78
00:04:07,933 --> 00:04:09,166
-Yeah.
-I love it, man.

79
00:04:09,233 --> 00:04:11,733
-So, you're a fan also of your--
-You gotta be a fan.

80
00:04:11,933 --> 00:04:13,233
-Yeah.
-Gotta be a fan.

81
00:04:15,033 --> 00:04:17,066
[Jerry] When you have a set
that you don't like,

82
00:04:17,133 --> 00:04:19,200
what do you do
to make yourself feel better?

83
00:04:19,266 --> 00:04:21,966
I'll stop by and get
a pint of Häagen-Dazs.

84
00:04:22,033 --> 00:04:24,066
Yeah, I've done that. Mint Chip.

85
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:25,400
You're a Mint Chip guy.

86
00:04:25,466 --> 00:04:27,099
Know what? You look like a Mint Chip guy.

87
00:04:27,166 --> 00:04:28,500
Mine is Butter Pecan.

88
00:04:28,566 --> 00:04:31,866
But I don't chew any of the pecans
until I finish the ice cream.

89
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,500
-But you know how--
-Boy, people are so strange.

90
00:04:34,766 --> 00:04:36,866
So, you save the pecan for last,

91
00:04:37,099 --> 00:04:41,133
even though there's no way
a pecan can follow an ice cream.

92
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:45,033
If you were putting together a show,
the ice cream is gonna close the show.

93
00:04:45,099 --> 00:04:46,366
The pecan is gonna open.

94
00:04:46,433 --> 00:04:48,666
I like how you think like a stand-up.

95
00:04:48,733 --> 00:04:51,166
-That's my life.
-The ice cream is the opener.

96
00:04:51,266 --> 00:04:53,033
Ice cream always closes.

97
00:04:53,099 --> 00:04:54,733
Nobody wants to follow ice cream.

98
00:04:57,233 --> 00:04:58,866
Most and very important to me about a car

99
00:04:58,933 --> 00:05:02,166
is the actuation of
all the knobs and levers.

100
00:05:02,266 --> 00:05:05,466
I want you to feel
putting this in reverse, how nice it is.

101
00:05:05,533 --> 00:05:07,166
Hold that button. Yeah, and then...

102
00:05:07,433 --> 00:05:09,533
Isn't that nice? The sound too, I like.

103
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:11,333
-Yeah. Yeah.
-A nice click sound. Listen.

104
00:05:13,533 --> 00:05:15,500
You know when it clicks, it's locked in.

105
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:18,099
Here's another thing, J.B.,
the door notches.

106
00:05:18,166 --> 00:05:20,966
Watch it goes up one,
and look at how wide it goes,

107
00:05:21,066 --> 00:05:24,000
-even wider, really easy.
-Oh!

108
00:05:24,833 --> 00:05:26,000
How do you like that?

109
00:05:26,066 --> 00:05:28,733
Know what I do with my car? I baby my car.

110
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:32,966
A lot of people use, uh,
towels and rags to wash their car.

111
00:05:33,232 --> 00:05:34,667
-Know what I wash my car with?
-What?

112
00:05:34,734 --> 00:05:35,601
Lingerie.

113
00:05:36,436 --> 00:05:37,370
[exhales]

114
00:05:37,966 --> 00:05:39,166
Ladies' lingerie.

115
00:05:39,233 --> 00:05:41,000
I soap it up, and I'll wash my car.

116
00:05:41,066 --> 00:05:42,099
Ladies' lingerie.

117
00:05:42,266 --> 00:05:44,066
Know why? 'Cause it's very delicate.

118
00:05:44,133 --> 00:05:47,099
The thing that makes me
the most uncomfortable about this

119
00:05:47,166 --> 00:05:50,133
is that you have lowered your voice
and you're whispering this to me.

120
00:05:50,233 --> 00:05:51,500
-I'm setting the mood.
-I know.

121
00:05:51,566 --> 00:05:53,533
That's making me uncomfortable, too.

122
00:05:53,886 --> 00:05:55,822
[inaudible]

123
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:08,099
-Oh.
-Man, that's good.

124
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:12,766
I lived in the projects in Mount Vernon.

125
00:06:12,833 --> 00:06:14,233
-In the projects?
-Yeah, man.

126
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:15,333
I lost a lot of friends.

127
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:17,933
I got friends who didn't
make it past 16 years old,

128
00:06:18,033 --> 00:06:20,066
so, you know, it's a great city.

129
00:06:20,133 --> 00:06:22,299
Hold on a second.
That sentence does not flow.

130
00:06:22,366 --> 00:06:24,633
"I lost a lot of friends
before the age of 16..."

131
00:06:24,766 --> 00:06:26,000
-Yeah.
-"It's a great city."

132
00:06:29,522 --> 00:06:30,623
[J.B.] You know what I love?

133
00:06:30,833 --> 00:06:33,933
When people laugh at the premise
before you even get to the punchline.

134
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,466
-It's the greatest.
-And you say to yourself, "Oh, you wait."

135
00:06:37,533 --> 00:06:41,366
When the premise gets something,
that's the greatest thing in comedy.

136
00:06:41,433 --> 00:06:43,133
I had a bit about chopsticks.

137
00:06:43,433 --> 00:06:45,033
The beginning of the premise was,

138
00:06:45,166 --> 00:06:48,066
"I see the Chinese are hanging
in there with the chopsticks..."

139
00:06:48,166 --> 00:06:49,266
[both laughing]

140
00:06:51,233 --> 00:06:52,833
I haven't even started the bit.

141
00:06:55,099 --> 00:06:56,500
Now let me tell you what lazy is.

142
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:58,000
The straw.

143
00:06:58,066 --> 00:07:00,333
You're two inches away from what you want.

144
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:02,766
That much space
between the glass and the straw.

145
00:07:02,866 --> 00:07:04,400
Do you need that help?

146
00:07:04,466 --> 00:07:05,866
Sometimes you can't find it. Like...

147
00:07:05,933 --> 00:07:07,533
Nobody looks good trying to get a straw.

148
00:07:07,599 --> 00:07:09,266
-You try to talk to people like...
-Yeah.

149
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:13,333
What's it for?
Is it people think the glass is dirty?

150
00:07:13,399 --> 00:07:15,168
You're drinking what's in the glass.

151
00:07:19,038 --> 00:07:21,507
I think at some point,
the zombie apocalypse is gonna happen.

152
00:07:21,633 --> 00:07:23,433
Now, just to refresh my memory,

153
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:26,000
the zombies,
they're dead and now they're back?

154
00:07:26,233 --> 00:07:28,099
-They're undead.
-And you like this.

155
00:07:28,166 --> 00:07:29,766
Yeah. Come back from the dead.

156
00:07:29,833 --> 00:07:31,033
But I do...

157
00:07:31,299 --> 00:07:32,666
[both laughing]

158
00:07:32,900 --> 00:07:36,266
-So what's your favorite zombie show?
-Walking Dead, man.

159
00:07:36,333 --> 00:07:37,366
I've never seen that.

160
00:07:37,433 --> 00:07:38,900
-Jerry.
-I'm sorry.

161
00:07:38,958 --> 00:07:41,694
-If you don't jump on Walking Dead...
-I'm sorry.

162
00:07:41,761 --> 00:07:43,763
I'm almost disgusted
that you have not yet--

163
00:07:43,830 --> 00:07:45,832
-I know, I know.
-People have told you, right?

164
00:07:45,898 --> 00:07:49,368
You're not the first person to be upset
with me for not watching their show.

165
00:07:49,435 --> 00:07:50,837
-Their show.
-This is another thing

166
00:07:50,903 --> 00:07:52,371
we need to stop doing to each other.

167
00:07:52,438 --> 00:07:55,441
-I cannot believe--
-I can't believe you missed The Grifters.

168
00:07:55,508 --> 00:07:57,410
-What's wrong with you?
-"What's wrong with you?"

169
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:00,966
That bothers me more
than the zombies trying to eat my brain.

170
00:08:05,333 --> 00:08:08,066
I worked at this perfume company,
man, for a few years.

171
00:08:08,133 --> 00:08:11,400
This guy taught me how to mix perfumes.

172
00:08:11,566 --> 00:08:12,966
I knew everything.

173
00:08:13,033 --> 00:08:14,200
I knew the ingredients.

174
00:08:14,266 --> 00:08:16,200
Lavenders and rosemarys.

175
00:08:16,266 --> 00:08:17,733
I could have been a perfumer.

176
00:08:18,066 --> 00:08:19,433
You know? Very easily.

177
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:23,266
You look like a guy
who started out in the perfume industry.

178
00:08:23,466 --> 00:08:25,166
You look like you're still in it, frankly.

179
00:08:28,866 --> 00:08:32,699
I used to be a waiter.
I was doing stand-up for free at night.

180
00:08:32,966 --> 00:08:35,766
And I would work as a waiter
from 10:00 a.m. to 2:00 p.m.

181
00:08:35,866 --> 00:08:38,633
I always did the lunch rush.
And a couple of times, J.B.,

182
00:08:38,699 --> 00:08:41,066
I walked up to a table
and they looked up at me and said,

183
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:45,157
"I saw you on stage last night. I thought
those were professional comedians."

184
00:08:45,233 --> 00:08:48,533
And I would just have to go,
"Well, not yet."

185
00:08:48,699 --> 00:08:50,199
[both laughing]

186
00:08:50,666 --> 00:08:51,900
I love that.

187
00:08:51,964 --> 00:08:54,834
But that prepares you, though.
You were dealing with people directly.

188
00:08:55,833 --> 00:08:57,566
Who's not dealing with people directly?

189
00:09:02,166 --> 00:09:04,033
-Is that a real bow tie?
-It's a real bow tie.

190
00:09:04,100 --> 00:09:06,466
It's beautifully tied. That knot is tight.

191
00:09:06,533 --> 00:09:09,833
Have you ever heard of
the Bow Tie Exchange Program?

192
00:09:09,949 --> 00:09:11,851
-No.
-Let me tell you about this world.

193
00:09:11,933 --> 00:09:13,766
If I have my bow tie on, right?

194
00:09:14,186 --> 00:09:17,056
Suppose another guy was sitting there.
He had a fancy bow tie.

195
00:09:17,133 --> 00:09:19,699
If you like my bow tie
and I like your bow tie,

196
00:09:19,766 --> 00:09:22,666
sometimes we'll meet,
and I'll say, "Hey, nice tie."

197
00:09:22,866 --> 00:09:24,033
Say, "Yeah, I like yours too."

198
00:09:24,100 --> 00:09:26,699
It's a certain energy
and a look we give each other,

199
00:09:26,833 --> 00:09:32,300
and what happens is you untie your tie,
he unties his tie, and you exchange ties.

200
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:36,066
This sounds like the way
homosexuality was conducted in the '50s.

201
00:09:38,699 --> 00:09:41,966
-Now, J.B., the "J" stands for "Jerry."
-Jerry.

202
00:09:42,033 --> 00:09:44,066
-We are the Jerrys.
-Okay?

203
00:09:44,199 --> 00:09:47,900
I think all Jerrys
look a little bit alike.

204
00:09:48,366 --> 00:09:52,733
And I would say further,
I think you and I look a little bit alike.

205
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:54,066
We do. I agree.

206
00:09:54,366 --> 00:09:56,000
Absolutely agree.

207
00:09:56,066 --> 00:09:57,433
-Really?
-Yeah!

208
00:10:01,066 --> 00:10:03,600
This fly is just ruining my life.

209
00:10:03,666 --> 00:10:07,233
I once saw a short film that was--
somebody attempted

210
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:09,600
to make the point of view of a fly.

211
00:10:09,742 --> 00:10:12,545
That's a wild ride.
If you could get a camera small enough,

212
00:10:12,633 --> 00:10:16,033
and I bet you someday they will
have a camera they can mount on a fly.

213
00:10:16,182 --> 00:10:19,151
-Need permission from a fly to put it on.
-You can hold him down.

214
00:10:19,218 --> 00:10:21,654
Yeah, but he still has to... He may...

215
00:10:21,721 --> 00:10:23,389
Know what I mean?
It may be uncomfortable.

216
00:10:23,466 --> 00:10:24,466
I don't care.

217
00:10:24,666 --> 00:10:25,766
[laughing]

218
00:10:26,466 --> 00:10:27,500
[Jerry] Thank you.

219
00:10:29,933 --> 00:10:32,166
-"I don't care."
-I'm running this thing, not the fly.

220
00:10:35,033 --> 00:10:36,033
I love bees.

221
00:10:36,100 --> 00:10:37,433
It's like human society,

222
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:39,866
but they don't have the problems
of human society.

223
00:10:40,233 --> 00:10:42,699
The worker bee doesn't
look at the drone and go,

224
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:45,533
-"I should be doing that."
-You're right.

225
00:10:45,633 --> 00:10:47,133
[both laughing]

226
00:10:50,233 --> 00:10:52,633
I used to sell fire extinguishers
door-to-door,

227
00:10:52,966 --> 00:10:54,800
which is the most terrible...

228
00:10:57,466 --> 00:11:00,933
Selling fire extinguishers door-to-door
is an art.

229
00:11:01,733 --> 00:11:03,800
For sure. It's one of the highest arts.

230
00:11:03,966 --> 00:11:07,866
-What was your most effective fear tactic?
-I mean, it's creepy.

231
00:11:08,166 --> 00:11:12,766
You open the door, I lean slightly...
just to peek in a little bit.

232
00:11:13,199 --> 00:11:14,333
Then I lean back up to see

233
00:11:14,400 --> 00:11:16,466
if I can see anything in the background
or hear kids.

234
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:17,866
-Good.
-Hear kids...

235
00:11:18,010 --> 00:11:21,380
If you're a fire extinguisher salesman,
the best thing to see on the floor

236
00:11:21,447 --> 00:11:22,548
-is toys.
-Is toys.

237
00:11:22,615 --> 00:11:24,750
I imagine there's a lot of "God forbid"
in that pitch.

238
00:11:24,833 --> 00:11:25,833
Oh, my God.

239
00:11:25,900 --> 00:11:28,233
"God forbid,
but if that happens, Mr. Johnson..."

240
00:11:28,287 --> 00:11:30,890
Yeah. So I gotta get in your house,
number one.

241
00:11:31,100 --> 00:11:33,166
Once I get in,
I'll start doing my demonstration.

242
00:11:33,225 --> 00:11:35,928
So I get in the house, I said,
"I'm gonna show you a demonstration."

243
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,733
I took my pan out, tear the paper up.
I light it up. [mimics explosion]

244
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:42,500
But Jerry, you gotta realize
I must have done 100 demonstrations.

245
00:11:42,766 --> 00:11:45,533
I didn't get a new sample
fire extinguisher for testing.

246
00:11:45,633 --> 00:11:47,133
-Oh, no.
-So...

247
00:11:47,199 --> 00:11:48,233
Oh, no.

248
00:11:48,300 --> 00:11:50,199
Jerry, Jerry,
I started a fire in a man's house.

249
00:11:50,433 --> 00:11:52,733
I went to use this fire extinguisher,
it would not work.

250
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:53,966
That's a great story.

251
00:11:54,033 --> 00:11:57,500
"How did the house burn down?"
"A fire extinguisher salesman."

252
00:12:01,033 --> 00:12:02,766
-Hi.
-[woman] It's my birthday today.

253
00:12:02,833 --> 00:12:04,199
Happy birthday!

254
00:12:04,300 --> 00:12:06,066
-What's your name?
-Eighty-five. Jeannie.

255
00:12:06,133 --> 00:12:07,366
Jeannie. Happy birthday.

256
00:12:07,433 --> 00:12:09,166
-Eighty-five. God bless you.
-Eighty-five!

257
00:12:09,233 --> 00:12:13,033
Did you hear about the two prostitutes
talking to one another

258
00:12:13,100 --> 00:12:15,500
and one says, "Do you smoke after sex?"

259
00:12:15,566 --> 00:12:17,533
She says, "I don't know, I never looked."

260
00:12:17,966 --> 00:12:19,266
[both laughing]

261
00:12:20,666 --> 00:12:21,766
That's a good one.

262
00:12:21,833 --> 00:12:25,300
You should put on some brighter colors
on an 85th birthday.

263
00:12:25,344 --> 00:12:27,413
Are you in the Bow Tie Exchange Program?

264
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:30,249
Do you want to exchange your bow tie
for her death pendant?

265
00:12:30,333 --> 00:12:31,533
-I would love to.
-No, no, no.

266
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:32,866
My pendant is very old.

267
00:12:32,933 --> 00:12:33,933
-I love that...
-Really?

268
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:35,400
Is that from the Titanic?

269
00:12:37,456 --> 00:12:38,891
[inaudible]

270
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:47,500
[Jerry] Look at the sheep.

271
00:12:47,566 --> 00:12:49,800
They're all looking at us
like we're looking at them.

272
00:12:49,866 --> 00:12:51,533
They're going, "Look at those two guys."

273
00:12:51,633 --> 00:12:54,066
This is where
they got the idea of Jar Jar Binks.

274
00:12:54,333 --> 00:12:57,433
They had one good joke
in seven Star Wars movies,

275
00:12:57,500 --> 00:13:00,066
when Jar Jar Binks says, "Exsqueeze me."

276
00:13:01,500 --> 00:13:02,633
That was the best line.

277
00:13:02,800 --> 00:13:04,766
Do you think this looks like you and me?

278
00:13:04,833 --> 00:13:07,500
It's very close, matter of fact.

279
00:13:07,699 --> 00:13:08,733
[both laughing]

280
00:13:13,159 --> 00:13:15,294
[J.B.] You ever been to a bathroom
with a trough?

281
00:13:15,366 --> 00:13:17,966
-Yes.
-That's something that I don't like.

282
00:13:18,033 --> 00:13:19,199
I don't like it either.

283
00:13:19,266 --> 00:13:21,266
Even though you don't really care

284
00:13:21,333 --> 00:13:23,166
what happens to your pee
once it leaves you.

285
00:13:23,233 --> 00:13:24,233
You don't care.

286
00:13:24,300 --> 00:13:27,500
But still, I don't want it joining
everybody else's pee,

287
00:13:27,566 --> 00:13:29,433
right here, right now.

288
00:13:32,978 --> 00:13:38,050
[Jerry] I'm happy to be talking to you
today as a fellow automobile person.

289
00:13:38,333 --> 00:13:40,699
What is it about driving a car?

290
00:13:40,900 --> 00:13:43,633
I mean, I just love turning this wheel
and then the car turns!

291
00:13:43,699 --> 00:13:46,199
-People like going places, man.
-Right.

292
00:13:46,258 --> 00:13:49,161
But a classic car enhances that even more.

293
00:13:49,228 --> 00:13:51,630
[Jerry] Yes, it does, because
there's a little time traveling.

294
00:13:51,699 --> 00:13:53,099
[J.B.] Little time traveling.

295
00:13:53,166 --> 00:13:55,533
-Right now it's 1964.
-Right.

296
00:13:55,599 --> 00:14:01,433
Sure, I can't drink out of every
water fountain right now, but it's 1964!

297
00:14:06,333 --> 00:14:10,500
A certain thing I don't trust
getting from people...

298
00:14:10,566 --> 00:14:15,666
A delivery of milk from the milk company
to my house, to my porch.

299
00:14:15,733 --> 00:14:17,633
-I don't trust...
-But they refrigerate it.

300
00:14:17,699 --> 00:14:22,300
If the person delivering the milk to me
was a cow...

301
00:14:22,528 --> 00:14:23,429
Ah.

302
00:14:23,633 --> 00:14:26,466
I'd rather have the cow himself
pull up and milk himself

303
00:14:26,533 --> 00:14:28,933
into the glass and give it to me.

304
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:30,766
I'll tell ya, a cow milking himself

305
00:14:30,833 --> 00:14:33,633
might not be the first thing
you want to see in the morning.

306
00:14:34,033 --> 00:14:35,466
[J.B. laughing]

307
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:44,066
Let's take the sound pack
out of your pocket.

308
00:14:44,133 --> 00:14:47,300
It's out of my pocket
because the last thing I want to do

309
00:14:47,366 --> 00:14:50,699
is go on a drug deal
and they ask me if I'm wired,

310
00:14:50,833 --> 00:14:53,133
and I say, "No, I'm not wired."
Say, "What's that?"

311
00:14:53,466 --> 00:14:56,000
[both laughing]

